(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test12_2A"] = [{"id":"2c50ed1a","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Literary scholars have struggled with the vastness of Nigerian writer Wole Soyinka’s collective works of drama (spanning over 20 plays in total). It is best, however, to understand Soyinka’s body of work as a dramatist chronologically. Soyinka’s progression as a playwright can be considered to fall into three periods, with each one representing a particular thematic and stylistic cohesion: the 1960s, the two decades between 1970 and 1990, and lastly, from roughly 1990 onwards. Text 2 It is tempting to impose a linear sense of order on the expanse of Wole Soyinka’s body of work as a dramatist. However, critics who have considered Soyinka’s plays to fit neatly into three phases overlook potential commonalities in Soyinka’s work that span across these phases. Additionally, this view may discount significant differences in the styles and content of plays written around the same time.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Literary scholars have struggled with the vastness of Nigerian writer Wole Soyinka’s collective works of drama (spanning over 20 plays in total). It","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"is best, however, to understand Soyinka’s body of work as a dramatist chronologically. Soyinka’s progression as a playwright can be considered","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"to fall into three periods, with each one representing a particular thematic and stylistic cohesion: the 1960s, the two decades between 1970 and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"1990, and lastly, from roughly 1990 onwards.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"It is tempting to impose a linear sense of order on the expanse of Wole Soyinka’s body of work as a dramatist. However, critics who have","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"considered Soyinka’s plays to fit neatly into three phases overlook potential commonalities in Soyinka’s work that span across these phases.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Additionally, this view may discount significant differences in the styles and content of plays written around the same time.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes a difference in how the author of Text 1 and the author of Text 2 view the study of Soyinka’s works of drama?","choices":["While the author of Text 1 believes that thinking about Soyinka’s works of theater in phases is useful, the author of Text 2 views such an approach as limiting.","Although the author of Text 1 claims that Soyinka’s style as a dramatist has evolved over time, the author of Text 2 argues that Soyinka’s style has remained consistent throughout his career.","The author of Text 1 considers Soyinka’s plays to showcase his strongest writing, whereas the author of Text 2 believes that Soyinka’s poetry is where he is most skilled.","The author of Text 1 argues that Soyinka’s early plays were his most politically charged, whereas the author of Text 2 claims that Soyinka’s most recent plays are the most politicized."]},{"id":"27d9bb69","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Many studies in psychology have shown that people seek out information even when they know in advance that they have no immediate use for it and that they won’t directly benefit from it. Such findings support the consensus view among researchers of curiosity: namely, that curiosity is not instrumental but instead represents a drive to acquire information for its own sake. Text 2 While acknowledging that acquiring information is a powerful motivator, Rachit Dubey and colleagues ran an experiment to test whether emphasizing the usefulness of scientific information could increase curiosity about it. They found that when research involving rats and fruit flies was presented as having medical applications for humans, participants expressed greater interest in learning about it than when the research was not presented as useful.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Many studies in psychology have shown that people seek out information even when they know in advance that they have no immediate use for","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"it and that they won’t directly benefit from it. Such findings support the consensus view among researchers of curiosity: namely, that curiosity is","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"not instrumental but instead represents a drive to acquire information for its own sake.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"While acknowledging that acquiring information is a powerful motivator, Rachit Dubey and colleagues ran an experiment to test whether","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"emphasizing the usefulness of scientific information could increase curiosity about it. They found that when research involving rats and fruit flies","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"was presented as having medical applications for humans, participants expressed greater interest in learning about it than when the research","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"was not presented as useful.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would Dubey and colleagues (Text 2) most likely respond to the consensus view discussed in Text 1?","choices":["By suggesting that curiosity may not be exclusively motivated by the desire to merely acquire information","By conceding that people may seek out information that serves no immediate purpose only because they think they can use it later","By pointing out that it is challenging to determine when information-seeking serves no goal beyond acquiring information","By disputing the idea that curiosity can help explain apparently purposeless information-seeking behaviors"]},{"id":"b4c6cff6","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is adapted from Karel Čapek’s 1920 play R.U.R. (Rossum’s Universal Robots), translated by Paul Selver and Nigel Playfair in 1923. Fabry and Busman are telling Miss Glory why their company manufactures robots. FABRY: One Robot can replace two and a half workmen. The human machine, Miss Glory, was terribly imperfect. It had to be removed sooner or later. BUSMAN: It was too expensive. FABRY: It was not effective. It no longer answers the requirements of modern engineering. Nature has no idea of keeping pace with modern labor. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Karel Čapek’s 1920 play R.U.R. (Rossum’s Universal Robots), translated by Paul Selver and Nigel Playfair in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"1923. Fabry and Busman are telling Miss Glory why their company manufactures robots.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"FABRY: One Robot can replace two and a half workmen. The human machine, Miss Glory, was terribly imperfect. It had to be removed sooner or","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"later.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"BUSMAN: It was too expensive.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"FABRY: It was not effective. It no longer answers the requirements of modern engineering. Nature has no idea of keeping pace with modern","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"labor.","indent":0}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “answers” most nearly mean?","choices":["Explains","Rebuts","Defends","Fulfills"],"underline":"answers","underline_segments":["answers"]},{"id":"ba974387","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is adapted from Mary Seacole’s 1857 autobiography Wonderful Adventures of Mrs. Seacole in Many Lands. That journey across the Isthmus [of Panama], insignificant in distance as it was, was by no means an easy one. It seemed as if nature had determined to throw every conceivable obstacle in the way of those who should seek to join the two great oceans of the world. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Mary Seacole’s 1857 autobiography Wonderful Adventures of Mrs. Seacole in Many Lands.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"That journey across the Isthmus [of Panama], insignificant in distance as it was, was by no means an easy one. It seemed as if","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"nature had determined to throw every conceivable obstacle in the way of those who should seek to join the two great oceans of","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"the world.","indent":2}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “conceivable” most nearly mean?","choices":["Obvious","Imaginable","Straightforward","Uninterrupted"],"underline":"conceivable","underline_segments":["conceivable"]},{"id":"4830ab87","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The way in which individual elements are balanced within a photographic image tends to affect how viewers perceive it: symmetry tends to give the elements equal importance, asymmetry emphasizes differences, and radial balance (organizing the elements around a central point) emphasizes the center over the periphery. What a photograph conveys is therefore largely ______ how it is balanced.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The way in which individual elements are balanced within a photographic image tends to affect how viewers perceive it: symmetry tends to give","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the elements equal importance, asymmetry emphasizes differences, and radial balance (organizing the elements around a central point)","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"emphasizes the center over the periphery. What a photograph conveys is therefore largely ______ how it is balanced.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["inhibited by","contingent on","obligated to","reserved for"]},{"id":"acb852e7","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is from the 1923 poem “Black Finger” by Angelina Weld Grimké, a Black American writer. A cypress is a type of evergreen tree. I have just seen a most beautiful thing, Slim and still, Against a gold, gold sky, A straight black cypress, Sensitive, Exquisite, A black finger Pointing upwards. Why, beautiful still finger, are you black? And why are you pointing upwards?","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from the 1923 poem “Black Finger” by Angelina Weld Grimké, a Black American writer. A cypress is a type of evergreen tree.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"I have just seen a most beautiful thing,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Slim and still,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Against a gold, gold sky,","indent":1},{"type":"line","text":"A straight black cypress,","indent":1},{"type":"line","text":"Sensitive,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Exquisite,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"A black finger","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Pointing upwards.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Why, beautiful still finger, are you black?","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"And why are you pointing upwards?","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["The speaker assesses a natural phenomenon, then questions the accuracy of her assessment.","The speaker describes a distinctive sight in nature, then ponders what meaning to attribute to that sight.","The speaker presents an outdoor scene, then considers a human behavior occurring within that scene.","The speaker examines her surroundings, then speculates about their influence on her emotional state."]},{"id":"1374a9fa","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Austin McCoy has argued that historians have tended to ______ rank-and-file workers in analyses of changes in organized labor in the United States in the decades following the Second World War, presenting political, business, and labor leaders as the major actors and ordinary workers as nearly devoid of agency.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Austin McCoy has argued that historians have tended to ______ rank-and-file workers in analyses of changes in organized labor in the United","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"States in the decades following the Second World War, presenting political, business, and labor leaders as the major actors and ordinary workers","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"as nearly devoid of agency.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["epitomize","marginalize","extol","substantiate"]},{"id":"12d81fc1","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Because literacy in Nahuatl script, the writing system of the Aztec Empire, was lost after Spain invaded central Mexico in the 1500s, it is unclear exactly how meaning was encoded in the script’s symbols. Although many scholars had assumed that the symbols signified entire words, linguist Alfonso Lacadena theorized in 2008 that they signified units of language smaller than words: individual syllables. Text 2 The growing consensus among scholars of Nahuatl script is that many of its symbols could signify either words or syllables, depending on syntax and content at any given site within a text. For example, the symbol signifying the word huipil (blouse) in some contexts could signify the syllable “pil” in others, as in the place name “Chipiltepec.” Thus, for the Aztecs, reading required a determination of how such symbols functioned each time they appeared in a text.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Because literacy in Nahuatl script, the writing system of the Aztec Empire, was lost after Spain invaded central Mexico in the 1500s, it is unclear","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"exactly how meaning was encoded in the script’s symbols. Although many scholars had assumed that the symbols signified entire words,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"linguist Alfonso Lacadena theorized in 2008 that they signified units of language smaller than words: individual syllables.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The growing consensus among scholars of Nahuatl script is that many of its symbols could signify either words or syllables, depending on","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"syntax and content at any given site within a text. For example, the symbol signifying the word huipil (blouse) in some contexts could signify the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"syllable “pil” in others, as in the place name “Chipiltepec.” Thus, for the Aztecs, reading required a determination of how such symbols functioned","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"each time they appeared in a text.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely characterize Lacadena’s theory, as described in Text 1?","choices":["By praising the theory for recognizing that the script’s symbols could represent entire words","By arguing that the theory is overly influenced by the work of earlier scholars","By approving of the theory’s emphasis on how the script changed over time","By cautioning that the theory overlooks certain important aspects of how the script functioned"]},{"id":"cf956802","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"When fashion designer Lloyd Henri Kiva New opened his store in Scottsdale, Arizona, in 1945, he quickly became known for creating delicately crafted leather goods, like belts and hats. He was perhaps most renowned for his colorful handbags, which he made by hand using a long and painstaking process. As he gained more customers, New began using sewing machines and other tools to help him produce bags more efficiently, though he continued to handcraft the crucial details that made each bag unique. Based on the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"When fashion designer Lloyd Henri Kiva New opened his store in Scottsdale, Arizona, in 1945, he quickly became known for creating delicately","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"crafted leather goods, like belts and hats. He was perhaps most renowned for his colorful handbags, which he made by hand using a long and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"painstaking process. As he gained more customers, New began using sewing machines and other tools to help him produce bags more","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"efficiently, though he continued to handcraft the crucial details that made each bag unique.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the text, what would have been the most likely consequence if New had not begun using sewing machines?","choices":["He would have been unable to ensure that each bag included unique, handcrafted details.","He would have struggled to meet the increasing demand for his bags.","He would have had to individually design each bag he produced.","He would not have been able to generate as much interest in his bags."]},{"id":"cbecb873","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Body Length, Filter Time, and Lunges per Dive for Four Whale Species Whale Typical adult body length Average time to filter all engulfed water Average number of lunges per dive deeper than species (meters) (seconds) 50 meters fin 18 – 22 31.30 3.95 humpback 11 – 17 17.12 6.28 minke 7 – 10 8.88 7.48 blue 24 – 34 60.27 4.02 Some whale species practice lunge feeding, in which they lunge toward prey with their mouths open at wide angles, collect the prey and the surrounding water, and then filter out the water through baleen plates in their mouths. Although the volume of water engulfed increases with whales body length, the surface area of whales baleen plates, which influences the rate at which water can be filtered, does not increase with body length to the same degree, which helps explain why ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Body Length, Filter Time, and Lunges per Dive for Four Whale Species","headers":["Whale species","Typical adult body length (meters)","Average time to filter all engulfed water (seconds)","Average number of lunges per dive deeper than 50 meters"],"rows":[["fin","18–22","31.30","3.95"],["humpback","11–17","17.12","6.28"],["minke","7–10","8.88","7.48"],["blue","24–34","60.27","4.02"]]},{"type":"text","text":"Some whale species practice lunge feeding, in which they lunge toward prey with their mouths open at wide angles, collect the prey and the surrounding water, and then filter out the water through baleen plates in their mouths. Although the volume of water engulfed increases with whales body length, the surface area of whales baleen plates, which influences the rate at which water can be filtered, does not increase with body length to the same degree, which helps explain why ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","choices":["minke whales and humpback whales show similar average filter times.","humpback whales show an average of 6.28 lunges per dive.","fin whales show a longer average filter time than minke whales do.","blue whales show the longest average filter time and the highest average number of lunges per dive."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Body Length,","Filter Time, and Lunges per Dive for Four","Whale Species"]},{"id":"57485f5e","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is adapted from Johanna Spyri’s 1881 novel Heidi (translated by Elisabeth Stork in 1915). Eight-year-old Heidi and her friend’s grandmother are looking at some illustrated books. Heidi had come and was looking with wondering eyes at the splendid pictures in the large books, that Grandmama was showing her. Suddenly she screamed aloud, for there on the picture she saw a peaceful flock grazing on a green pasture. In the middle a shepherd was standing, leaning on his crook. The setting sun was shedding a golden light over everything. With glowing eyes Heidi devoured the scene.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Johanna Spyri’s 1881 novel Heidi (translated by Elisabeth Stork in 1915). Eight-year-old Heidi and her friend’s","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"grandmother are looking at some illustrated books.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Heidi had come and was looking with wondering eyes at the splendid pictures in the large books, that Grandmama was showing","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"her. Suddenly she screamed aloud, for there on the picture she saw a peaceful flock grazing on a green pasture. In the middle a","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"shepherd was standing, leaning on his crook. The setting sun was shedding a golden light over everything. With glowing eyes","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Heidi devoured the scene.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Heidi is upset until she sees a serene image of a pasture in one of Grandmama’s books.","Heidi is delighted and fascinated by an image she sees in one of Grandmama’s books.","Heidi is initially frightened by an image in one of Grandmama’s books but quickly comes to appreciate its beauty.","Heidi is inspecting an image in one of Grandmama’s books because she has never seen a shepherd with his sheep before."]},{"id":"a66f9b8d","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Cats can judge unseen people’s positions in space by the sound of their voices and thus react with surprise when the same person calls to them from two different locations in a short span of time. Saho Takagi and colleagues reached this conclusion by measuring cats’ levels of surprise","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Cats can judge unseen people’s positions in space by the sound of their voices and thus react with surprise when the same person calls to them","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"from two different locations in a short span of time. Saho Takagi and colleagues reached this conclusion by measuring cats’ levels of surprise","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"based on their ear and head movements while the cats heard recordings of their owners’ voices from two speakers spaced far apart. Cats","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"exhibited a low level of surprise when owners’ voices were played twice from the same speaker, but they showed a high level of surprise when","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the voice was played once each from the two different speakers.","indent":0}],"question":"According to the text, how did the researchers determine the level of surprise displayed by the cats in the study?","choices":["They watched how each cat moved its ears and head.","They examined how each cat reacted to the voice of a stranger.","They studied how each cat physically interacted with its owner.","They tracked how each cat moved around the room."]},{"id":"b633cc4f","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Few animals are known to spit: among them are humans, cobras, and camels. But in January 2022 at a nature preserve in southern England, bird- watcher Clare Jacobs observed a gray seal spitting a jet of water at a white-tailed eagle flying overhead. Seals had never been seen spitting before. Biologist Sean Twiss, who studies gray seals, believes that the seal may have been attempting to scare the eagle away from a food source or that the seal may have just been playing.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Few animals are known to spit: among them are humans, cobras, and camels. But in January 2022 at a nature preserve in southern England, bird-","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"watcher Clare Jacobs observed a gray seal spitting a jet of water at a white-tailed eagle flying overhead. Seals had never been seen spitting","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"before. Biologist Sean Twiss, who studies gray seals, believes that the seal may have been attempting to scare the eagle away from a food","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"source or that the seal may have just been playing.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main topic of the text?","choices":["Bird-watching in southern England","A previously unseen behavior of gray seals","How white-tailed eagles defend their territory against other predators","Differences between gray seals and white-tailed eagles"]},{"id":"b8199d5a","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Total Areas and 2022 Populations of Smallest Arabian Peninsula Countries Country Total area (square miles) Population Kuwait 6,880 4,268,873 Bahrain 304 1,472,233 Qatar 4,471 2,695,122 In terms of area and population, the three smallest Arabian Peninsula countries are Bahrain, Qatar, and Kuwait.","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Total Areas and 2022 Populations of Smallest Arabian Peninsula Countries","headers":["Country","Total area (square miles)","Population"],"rows":[["Kuwait","6,880","4,268,873"],["Bahrain","304","1,472,233"],["Qatar","4,471","2,695,122"]]},{"type":"text","text":"In terms of area and population, the three smallest Arabian Peninsula countries are Bahrain, Qatar, and Kuwait."}],"question":"According to the table, what is the total area of Bahrain?","choices":["4,268,873 square miles","4,471 square miles","304 square miles","6,880 square miles"],"underline":"Arabian Peninsula Countries","underline_segments":["Arabian Peninsula Countries"]},{"id":"61228830","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"A heliograph is a semaphore device used for sending optical communications —usually in the form of Morse code — by reflecting flashes of sunlight off a mirror. Heliographs were used for rapid communication across expansive distances for military, surveying, and forestry purposes during the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, but they were largely effective only during the daytime, and the range of the device depended on factors such as the opacity of the air and line of sight. Therefore, heliographs were eventually replaced by technology that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A heliograph is a semaphore device used for sending optical communications—usually in the form of Morse code—by reflecting flashes of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"sunlight off a mirror. Heliographs were used for rapid communication across expansive distances for military, surveying, and forestry purposes","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"during the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, but they were largely effective only during the daytime, and the range of the device","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"depended on factors such as the opacity of the air and line of sight. Therefore, heliographs were eventually replaced by technology that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["worked on similar principles but was easier to produce and maintain.","was not so constrained by environmental circumstances.","could be used for more than military, surveying, or forestry purposes.","enabled communication that didn’t require knowledge of Morse code."]},{"id":"8b017d4e","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Supported by biochemical analyses of over 2,000 skeletons from the Middle Ages, ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Supported by biochemical analyses of over 2,000 skeletons from the Middle Ages, ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["vegetables and grains were, a 2022 study found, the primary components of early medieval rulers’ diets.","early medieval rulers’ diets were found, in a 2022 study, to have primarily consisted of vegetables and grains.","the primary components of early medieval rulers’ diets were vegetables and grains, according to a 2022 study.","findings from a 2022 study suggested that vegetables and grains were the primary components of early medieval rulers’ diets."]},{"id":"6fdddabf","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"The part of a compound that determines the compound’s color is ______ the chromophore. One example of a chromophore is hemoglobin, which gives human blood its red color.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The part of a compound that determines the compound’s color is ______ the chromophore. One example of a chromophore is hemoglobin, which","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"gives human blood its red color.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["called,","called","called—","called;"]},{"id":"35360da9","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"The US Geological Survey wants to map every human-made structure in the United States, and it is asking volunteers to help. Cassie Tammy Wang and Ashish D’Souza are just two of the many volunteer map editors who ______ to the project since it began in 2012.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The US Geological Survey wants to map every human-made structure in the United States, and it is asking volunteers to help. Cassie Tammy","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Wang and Ashish D’Souza are just two of the many volunteer map editors who ______ to the project since it began in 2012.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["contribute","will contribute","have contributed","will be contributing"]},{"id":"f78997cf","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Recent analysis of 32532 Thereus — an outer solar system object orbiting the Sun between Jupiter and Saturn — has determined its color to be gray, suggesting an icy composition. Such interpretations are ultimately ______ the object’s gray coloration may be an incidental effect of radiation, solar wind, or collisions with other objects rather than evidence of its physical makeup.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Recent analysis of 32532 Thereus—an outer solar system object orbiting the Sun between Jupiter and Saturn—has determined its color to be","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"gray, suggesting an icy composition. Such interpretations are ultimately ______ the object’s gray coloration may be an incidental effect of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"radiation, solar wind, or collisions with other objects rather than evidence of its physical makeup.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["speculative, though","speculative, though;","speculative; though","speculative, though,"]},{"id":"ea0aa676","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"In the 1970s, Janaki Ammal, a prominent botanist, emerged as a powerful voice in India’s environmental conservation movement. Her exhaustive chromosomal survey of plants in Silent Valley, a pristine tropical forest in Kerala, India, that is home to nearly 1,000 species of native flora (many of which are endangered), ______ instrumental in the government’s decision to preserve the forest.","question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["are","were","have been","was"]},{"id":"aab78b25","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Psychophysicist Howard Moskowitz was hired by a soda company to determine how much artificial sweetener ______ After conducting consumer taste tests, he found that no such ideal existed: participants expressed a wide range of preferences for different blends of sweetener, carbonization, and flavoring.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Psychophysicist Howard Moskowitz was hired by a soda company to determine how much artificial sweetener ______ After conducting","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"consumer taste tests, he found that no such ideal existed: participants expressed a wide range of preferences for different blends of sweetener,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"carbonization, and flavoring.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["do most people prefer in a diet drink?","do most people prefer in a diet drink.","most people prefer in a diet drink?","most people prefer in a diet drink."]},{"id":"9fe7315b","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Few New Yorkers have heard Thelma Pollard’s name. ______ many have seen her work in the famous Broadway musical The Phantom of the Opera. As the musical’s longtime makeup supervisor, Pollard put the iconic makeup on the face of the Phantom himself.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Few New Yorkers have heard Thelma Pollard’s name. ______ many have seen her work in the famous Broadway musical The Phantom of the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Opera. As the musical’s longtime makeup supervisor, Pollard put the iconic makeup on the face of the Phantom himself.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Similarly,","Therefore,","For example,","However,"]},{"id":"35507eba","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Pointillism is a painting technique in which small, distinct dots of color are applied in patterns to form an image. Betty Acquah is an artist from Ghana who uses pointillism in her work. “By extending dabs of color in the subject matter into the background and vice-versa, an illusion of movement is created,” she says about pointillism. Her work often portrays Ghanaian women, whom she sees as the “unsung heroines of the Ghanaian Republic.” Her pointillist painting “Exquisite” (2016) features five dancing women twirling their skirts. The student wants to provide a quotation from Acquah that explains why she used pointillism in “Exquisite.”","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Pointillism is a painting technique in which small, distinct dots of color are applied in patterns to form an image.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Betty Acquah is an artist from Ghana who uses pointillism in her work.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"“By extending dabs of color in the subject matter into the background and vice-versa, an illusion of movement is created,” she says about","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"pointillism.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Her work often portrays Ghanaian women, whom she sees as the “unsung heroines of the Ghanaian Republic.”","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Her pointillist painting “Exquisite” (2016) features five dancing women twirling their skirts.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["In painting “Exquisite,” Acquah applied pointillism to create what she called an “illusion of movement” within the painting’s five dancing women and their twirling skirts.","Pointillism, the technique used in Acquah’s “Exquisite,” involves the application of small, distinct dots of color.","In “Exquisite,” Acquah uses a technique that she says involves “extending dabs of color in the subject matter into the background and vice- versa.”","“Exquisite” portrays Acquah’s fellow Ghanaian women as she sees them: the “unsung heroes of the Ghanaian Republic.”"]},{"id":"b7571c0a","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Practical movie effects, such as the use of actual locations in a film, provide a more realistic visual experience than computer-generated imagery (CGI) does, but giving audiences the “real thing” can be prohibitively expensive. ______ many filmmakers use a blended approach, employing practical effects whenever possible and CGI elements as necessary to control costs.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Practical movie effects, such as the use of actual locations in a film, provide a more realistic visual experience than computer-generated imagery","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"(CGI) does, but giving audiences the “real thing” can be prohibitively expensive. ______ many filmmakers use a blended approach, employing","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"practical effects whenever possible and CGI elements as necessary to control costs.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Similarly,","For this reason,","Furthermore,","In other words,"]},{"id":"264e7415","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: The Philadelphia and Lancaster Turnpike was a road built between 1792 and 1794. It was the first private turnpike in the United States. It connected the cities of Philadelphia and Lancaster in the state of Pennsylvania. It was sixty-two miles long. The student wants to emphasize the distance covered by the Philadelphia and Lancaster Turnpike.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The Philadelphia and Lancaster Turnpike was a road built between 1792 and 1794.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"It was the first private turnpike in the United States.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"It connected the cities of Philadelphia and Lancaster in the state of Pennsylvania.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"It was sixty-two miles long.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The sixty-two-mile-long Philadelphia and Lancaster Turnpike connected the Pennsylvania cities of Philadelphia and Lancaster.","The Philadelphia and Lancaster Turnpike was the first private turnpike in the United States.","The Philadelphia and Lancaster Turnpike, which connected two Pennsylvania cities, was built between 1792 and 1794.","A historic Pennsylvania road, the Philadelphia and Lancaster Turnpike was completed in 1794."]},{"id":"f5149550","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"A staunch supporter of women’s voting rights, Wilhelmina Kekelaokalaninui Widemann Dowsett sought to coordinate the efforts of suffragists in ‘ ‘ her native Hawai i. ______ in 1912, she founded the National Women’s Equal Suffrage Association of Hawai i, an organization that lobbied for women’s voting rights in the US territory.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A staunch supporter of women’s voting rights, Wilhelmina Kekelaokalaninui Widemann Dowsett sought to coordinate the efforts of suffragists in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"her native Hawai‘i. ______ in 1912, she founded the National Women’s Equal Suffrage Association of Hawai‘i, an organization that lobbied for","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"women’s voting rights in the US territory.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["In other words,","Conversely,","To that end,","Alternatively,"]},{"id":"92fe0ed7","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Geoscientists have long considered Hawaii’s Mauna Loa volcano to be Earth’s largest shield volcano by volume, measuring approximately 74,000 cubic kilometers. ______ according to a 2020 study by local geoscientist Michael Garcia, Hawaii’s P ūhāhonu shield volcano is significantly larger, boasting a volume of about 148,000 cubic kilometers.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Geoscientists have long considered Hawaii’s Mauna Loa volcano to be Earth’s largest shield volcano by volume, measuring approximately 74,000","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"cubic kilometers. ______ according to a 2020 study by local geoscientist Michael Garcia, Hawaii’s Pūhāhonu shield volcano is significantly larger,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"boasting a volume of about 148,000 cubic kilometers.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Secondly,","Consequently,","Moreover,","However,"]}];
